questions.q:
Priya claims: 'Bharata was already the usual name for the whole land in Emperor Ashoka's time, because the word Bharata is older than Ashoka and the Mahabharata had already used Bharatavarsha.' Evaluate her claim. In your answer:
(a) say where the name 'Bharata' first appears and what it refers to there;
(b) say what 'Bharatavarsha' means;
(c) name the term Ashoka used for the whole of India in his inscription of about 250 BCE;
(d) say when, according to the chapter, 'Bharata' became the name generally used for the Subcontinent, and which ancient text the chapter quotes for it;
(e) give your conclusion on Priya's claim.

Rukmini argues: 'The Constitution, first written in English, says "India, that is Bharat", putting India first. So the name India must be older than the name Bharata.' Evaluate her argument. In your answer:
(a) say how the Hindi version of the Constitution orders the two names, and what this shows about the order of words;
(b) name the earliest source in the chapter where the word Bharata appears, and what it referred to there;
(c) trace the name India back through the Persians and the Greeks;
(d) compare when the word Bharata and the Persian name Hindu first appear in the chapter's sources;
(e) give your conclusion on Rukmini's argument.
